f7-5f39-a6a6-5d1a9c67c2a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-120887" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $4\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\sqrt{5}=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\sqrt{5} & =0 \\\\ 4\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})={60}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${60}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={60}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/9c8adbb0a1594ae5bacfab7f1f92993c67659839599e10ab46d5cf98896e3ec7.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={300}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={60}^{∘},\\,{300}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is $60\\cdot 300=18\\,000.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-120887/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45745641-ac49-5b90-8ad6-86deaa05c609"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120887"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-338252/field-1/d569d64e1b94dabdc9f89c1070c848c6c96526a20f320d6b4971ae3a32604bcf" :answer/id #uuid "5dc7fb0a-26d6-5e24-a12b-7ba19c893cf4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338252/field-1/ccc5b7f3cfdb4c2dfc0e1f31be54559a1ab3adaf142e500fc1f1b7bc87e93b08" :answer/id #uuid "76c49a68-6813-51a8-8e23-1791f48f907a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338252/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "382877e9-1903-58fc-8257-1becd41b96b7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-338252/field-1/d569d64e1b94dabdc9f89c1070c848c6c96526a20f320d6b4971ae3a32604bcf" :answer-field/choices ["q-338252/field-1/d569d64e1b94dabdc9f89c1070c848c6c96526a20f320d6b4971ae3a32604bcf" "q-338252/field-1/ccc5b7f3cfdb4c2dfc0e1f31be54559a1ab3adaf142e500fc1f1b7bc87e93b08"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338252/field-2/52c87b0a682a19340b4e6e7cfe0374d7d2f0e6ecf0887e855bd23921720e9acc" :answer/id #uuid "6cf4b1a0-f7cb-5ca0-9a72-a5fcc0d1e5ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338252/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "e4c052b6-cd19-5785-b38c-c553f3e78e67" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-338252/field-2/52c87b0a682a19340b4e6e7cfe0374d7d2f0e6ecf0887e855bd23921720e9acc" :answer-field/choices ["q-338252/field-2/52c87b0a682a19340b4e6e7cfe0374d7d2f0e6ecf0887e855bd23921720e9acc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338252" :question/id #uuid "28eb9d47-7311-5c47-9e0a-827d0b6e01d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-338252" :question/problem "Arranged in **ascending order**, the solutions of the equation $4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2=0$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ are $x=${{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2 & =0 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-2 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/d188a8634c7d33813260472c8691dccdbedbc809d3b59445afc912a43a09675b.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions of the equation, arranged in ascending order, are $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-338252/field-1" "q-338252/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45745641-ac49-5b90-8ad6-86deaa05c609"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-338252"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-338230/field-1/a4c8bdda186d0d425e9963bf17967cd5e356d934f319fa58135b8f8cf8a589f8" :answer/id #uuid "9bae55fd-66c3-5627-ad93-3c45dba8ae9d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "30" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338230/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "f7a41e80-16b3-5ec5-8c63-ff0680d1c53d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-338230/field-1/a4c8bdda186d0d425e9963bf17967cd5e356d934f319fa58135b8f8cf8a589f8" :answer-field/choices ["q-338230/field-1/a4c8bdda186d0d425e9963bf17967cd5e356d934f319fa58135b8f8cf8a589f8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338230/field-2/6d5e653a0cc5c6e6fc44c5c3386727c89009586dc5171332dccb4c794ca30987" :answer/id #uuid "a878f681-8c62-5b05-9d86-60ebea245b41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "330" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338230/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "e8e55b6d-4d5a-5c49-811d-0c593663ca9a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-338230/field-2/6d5e653a0cc5c6e6fc44c5c3386727c89009586dc5171332dccb4c794ca30987" :answer-field/choices ["q-338230/field-2/6d5e653a0cc5c6e6fc44c5c3386727c89009586dc5171332dccb4c794ca30987"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338230" :question/id #uuid "2c3f6ee4-25da-5eee-9c39-e29f0b38f7d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-338230" :question/problem "Arranged in **ascending order**, the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ for ${0}^{∘}<x\\le {360}^{∘},$ are $x=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})={30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${30}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={30}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/d88379fcad6ee1bfc7f6c2159c814f92ca330dbacee191ebebb027bab306fe2c.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={330}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions of the equation, arranged in ascending order, are $x={30}^{∘}$ and $x={330}^{∘}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-338230/field-1" "q-338230/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45745641-ac49-5b90-8ad6-86deaa05c609"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-338230"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-338258/field-1/c89a78e01f135d27857f87b29786d3bde75753fd31e9ec00c0c39b58d64c0b12" :answer/id #uuid "bde5d558-ddf1-557b-a66c-c1b577381475" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "135" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338258/field-1/ca566c3861ad95266b92d3d8e36c85c39daf1d1780cc1bf826ae0ac7accce583" :answer/id #uuid "837173a3-804c-5ff0-93f2-69b9076f5d31" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "45" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338258/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "6c61f7af-be24-567e-b818-738d931aeb7b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-338258/field-1/c89a78e01f135d27857f87b29786d3bde75753fd31e9ec00c0c39b58d64c0b12" :answer-field/choices ["q-338258/field-1/c89a78e01f135d27857f87b29786d3bde75753fd31e9ec00c0c39b58d64c0b12" "q-338258/field-1/ca566c3861ad95266b92d3d8e36c85c39daf1d1780cc1bf826ae0ac7accce583"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338258/field-2/e9bf0d8683d697a9168d9766312e0d9acef296c2f3ae0228f832eaed69a30a71" :answer/id #uuid "c175c27d-4593-54ac-9856-1f5ed5d66330" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "225" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338258/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "ec6c6079-9291-5277-9128-14ed401ec4ca" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-338258/field-2/e9bf0d8683d697a9168d9766312e0d9acef296c2f3ae0228f832eaed69a30a71" :answer-field/choices ["q-338258/field-2/e9bf0d8683d697a9168d9766312e0d9acef296c2f3ae0228f832eaed69a30a71"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338258" :question/id #uuid "7e1b0798-16fd-5a51-a661-6bc33d230919" :question/math-academy-id "q-338258" :question/problem "Arranged in **ascending order**, the solutions of the equation $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}=\\sqrt{2}$ for ${0}^{∘}<x\\le {360}^{∘},$ are $x=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2} & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={135}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${135}^{∘}$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={135}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/144ed33e7739fbaf17e3f81dd9ae2fd0a2d8e4d622ced7cb5dd60c8d177b6c01.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={225}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions of the equation, arranged in ascending order, are $x={135}^{∘}$ and $x={225}^{∘}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-338258/field-1" "q-338258/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45745641-ac49-5b90-8ad6-86deaa05c609"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-338258"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-338261/field-1/d569d64e1b94dabdc9f89c1070c848c6c96526a20f320d6b4971ae3a32604bcf" :answer/id #uuid "66e1e48f-5de9-5688-9c3b-b18c9a6b6d68" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338261/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "2581334b-3434-5278-9cb9-7c38868112e1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-338261/field-1/d569d64e1b94dabdc9f89c1070c848c6c96526a20f320d6b4971ae3a32604bcf" :answer-field/choices ["q-338261/field-1/d569d64e1b94dabdc9f89c1070c848c6c96526a20f320d6b4971ae3a32604bcf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338261/field-2/52c87b0a682a19340b4e6e7cfe0374d7d2f0e6ecf0887e855bd23921720e9acc" :answer/id #uuid "fe6052cd-40ad-5cf7-a220-fe376a972c17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338261/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "ee6b764b-200b-50d8-b36a-bb7c31ead8be" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-338261/field-2/52c87b0a682a19340b4e6e7cfe0374d7d2f0e6ecf0887e855bd23921720e9acc" :answer-field/choices ["q-338261/field-2/52c87b0a682a19340b4e6e7cfe0374d7d2f0e6ecf0887e855bd23921720e9acc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338261" :question/id #uuid "90d8ea8b-4e0a-538b-bdbc-28f62f92d15a" :question/math-academy-id "q-338261" :question/problem "Arranged in **ascending order**, the solutions of the equation $6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4=1$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=${{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4 & =1 \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/d188a8634c7d33813260472c8691dccdbedbc809d3b59445afc912a43a09675b.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions of the equation, arranged in ascending order, are $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-338261/field-1" "q-338261/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45745641-ac49-5b90-8ad6-86deaa05c609"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-338261"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-338346/field-1/c258b70821560c3c05fcb4407c110ff76fb2be43ff09a851c52ebb0455132f20" :answer/id #uuid "b564c853-cd0c-5876-9953-e57252d54a9e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338346/field-1/4c51e084e6253614e5c4efd2c8489e6900ae5b8c4e1204f11752d4e103d95983" :answer/id #uuid "c03d072e-c84b-5934-85cd-1d03b1a2f39f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338346/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "0fe01312-4008-520e-9dbf-93cd685440bf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-338346/field-1/c258b70821560c3c05fcb4407c110ff76fb2be43ff09a851c52ebb0455132f20" :answer-field/choices ["q-338346/field-1/c258b70821560c3c05fcb4407c110ff76fb2be43ff09a851c52ebb0455132f20" "q-338346/field-1/4c51e084e6253614e5c4efd2c8489e6900ae5b8c4e1204f11752d4e103d95983"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338346/field-2/5e950aecb6598a883ffa82e3c3436a51ee77b93824c15eaedeb154a45b1fb771" :answer/id #uuid "2e8a2d91-6ab5-5263-ae40-3d6822cb5de8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338346/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "1e0a8909-a153-5887-9a3e-5917acdaca55" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-338346/field-2/5e950aecb6598a883ffa82e3c3436a51ee77b93824c15eaedeb154a45b1fb771" :answer-field/choices ["q-338346/field-2/5e950aecb6598a883ffa82e3c3436a51ee77b93824c15eaedeb154a45b1fb771"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338346" :question/id #uuid "6b6b30aa-3838-5b8c-8682-eaa8579ef511" :question/math-academy-id "q-338346" :question/problem "Arranged in **ascending order** and rounded to **one decimal place**, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{7}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=${{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{3}{7})=2.013\\,707…≈2.0137\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2.0137$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution, rounded to one decimal place, is $x=2.0.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of \n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -2.0137≈1.1279.\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.1279.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/157096afa9d3de44710dfa11c99f1f699815156fbcd5d649c1b4048dbbba9913.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +1.1279 \\\\  & ≈4.3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x=2.0$ and $x=4.3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-338346/field-1" "q-338346/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f607558-5a7f-52df-b344-57f2f84791e9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-338346"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-338422/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "755174b4-8da1-5917-81b3-079d52e3f4e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338422/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a6f34b66-0d44-5e56-bd44-8dc079a1dc71" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-338422/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-338422/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338422/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "19186045-6879-52ee-a9d6-a4cdb3e2e78a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338422/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "f756d634-f2e1-574c-8ff5-f47475f51b94" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-338422/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-338422/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338422" :question/id #uuid "f33d23f5-c525-57bc-85e7-c27a008eaf11" :question/math-academy-id "q-338422" :question/problem "Arranged in **ascending order** and rounded to **the nearest integer**, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{4}{9}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=${{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{4}{9})=2.031\\,350…≈2.031\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2.031$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution, rounded to the nearest integer, is $x=2.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of \n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -2.031≈1.111.\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.111.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/d416faad39ff978c5ad59b91ceee311d358818ee7bf486f0dea95b9ae6319978.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +1.111 \\\\  & ≈4.253 \\\\  & ≈4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x=2$ and $x=4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-338422/field-1" "q-338422/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f607558-5a7f-52df-b344-57f2f84791e9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-338422"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120981/selection/76c4fc174488a2dfcb2fd772cf3f97232b8e3aa256c18a493e6dd516bc975248" :answer/id #uuid "8ad4ba8b-800f-5955-ba4a-207118545c84" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120981/selection/59aed57f41e18b28e62913aef722072df30eb021fdbba674960b6ff4e4b74960" :answer/id #uuid "5d669ecf-8ecd-5d2e-b938-90cc3edd9d38" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4.1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120981/selection/c58eeab7803d45b65d22e79203add957fd21e5673d9833c3339ac6d325c6345f" :answer/id #uuid "23a6a3ae-7e93-542d-84bb-64d8fc67838b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120981/selection/d0d01bb2e3e816b9fd6aee47accd74e6df07f3f36fe536db4d7bfbcde2546d68" :answer/id #uuid "db2b665d-83a5-5f2f-9fe1-51ffb34fa5d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120981/selection/12421a6e1ec720412f010275bbc108feb3faf4606c642b7e436f211edc66b521" :answer/id #uuid "fd9d3da5-1997-5bf3-b3b5-16391da60a2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120981/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a33c0a9b-c9dd-5c35-bc30-e81cc341ab7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120981/selection/d0d01bb2e3e816b9fd6aee47accd74e6df07f3f36fe536db4d7bfbcde2546d68" :answer-field/choices ["q-120981/selection/76c4fc174488a2dfcb2fd772cf3f97232b8e3aa256c18a493e6dd516bc975248" "q-120981/selection/59aed57f41e18b28e62913aef722072df30eb021fdbba674960b6ff4e4b74960" "q-120981/selection/c58eeab7803d45b65d22e79203add957fd21e5673d9833c3339ac6d325c6345f" "q-120981/selection/d0d01bb2e3e816b9fd6aee47accd74e6df07f3f36fe536db4d7bfbcde2546d68" "q-120981/selection/12421a6e1ec720412f010275bbc108feb3faf4606c642b7e436f211edc66b521"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120981" :question/id #uuid "1b6ef5ea-e806-5bce-9fb5-8b166565989c" :question/math-academy-id "q-120981" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{9}{11}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{9}{11})=0.612\\,554…≈0.612\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0.612\\,6$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.612\\,6.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.612\\,6.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.612\\,6.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/23712114dae7013de5786e51b99cbd0857c41ed6443e0cb6a9f54aa4ba00dcbb.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -0.612\\,6 \\\\  & ≈5.670\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=0.612\\,6\\cdot 5.670\\,6≈3.5\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-120981/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f607558-5a7f-52df-b344-57f2f84791e9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120981"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-338342/field-1/e983a3acef4246594c7c60522bdb0d85ed6924356b3a7dae378b4cc95ed4f927" :answer/id #uuid "ca89b78a-382a-5f6f-a6ba-b7ea32a330b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338342/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a60be9e3-b9dd-50a3-9b00-4276c0c419be" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-338342/field-1/e983a3acef4246594c7c60522bdb0d85ed6924356b3a7dae378b4cc95ed4f927" :answer-field/choices ["q-338342/field-1/e983a3acef4246594c7c60522bdb0d85ed6924356b3a7dae378b4cc95ed4f927"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338342/field-2/0f8a5ebc257de695fa1c5a75ee3c56c49f0536f265649ed1f8190bfce872ed23" :answer/id #uuid "60127a9c-4f23-5299-b7cf-e508635eb41a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5.0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338342/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "ed5b4d4c-6794-5117-819a-a103c6236dfa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-338342/field-2/0f8a5ebc257de695fa1c5a75ee3c56c49f0536f265649ed1f8190bfce872ed23" :answer-field/choices ["q-338342/field-2/0f8a5ebc257de695fa1c5a75ee3c56c49f0536f265649ed1f8190bfce872ed23"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338342" :question/id #uuid "30d61a94-a432-5495-b43d-b92c98c3391b" :question/math-academy-id "q-338342" :question/problem "Arranged in **ascending order** and rounded to **one decimal place**, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{2}{7}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=${{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{2}{7})=1.281\\,044…≈1.2810\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1.2810$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution, rounded to one decimal place, is $x=1.3.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=1.2810.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.2810.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/1b1b50c4d13ebae81a4c8af45ec2c9fde8dc6810da78bd21f74ebcdd117a375e.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -1.2810 \\\\  & ≈5.0022 \\\\  & ≈5.0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x=1.3$ and $x=5.0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-338342/field-1" "q-338342/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f607558-5a7f-52df-b344-57f2f84791e9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-338342"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-72840/selection/66dedd51c696580d351ab6a45c1c63cb495081ccf219c3c3c62f1d90fbeb5a49" :answer/id #uuid "1f2f0d92-5024-564b-ac78-c223c04dc862" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72840/selection/478cbf1ea235fbfb5b60a0837fffa0a678f83a3bcb8f958a024b0760ea2175ce" :answer/id #uuid "ff1b4a88-404d-564c-941f-15cf03a7c73a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72840/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer/id #uuid "3f083f61-fe2f-517e-9056-2ae59d95d4aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72840/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer/id #uuid "111a4836-4164-50f9-a468-9835454d177e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72840/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "1c6ba047-7eb6-5624-b3da-6fc8295594c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72840/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "22abdfd4-164a-5e27-bbfa-11c150aab333" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-72840/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer-field/choices ["q-72840/selection/66dedd51c696580d351ab6a45c1c63cb495081ccf219c3c3c62f1d90fbeb5a49" "q-72840/selection/478cbf1ea235fbfb5b60a0837fffa0a678f83a3bcb8f958a024b0760ea2175ce" "q-72840/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" "q-72840/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" "q-72840/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-72840" :question/id #uuid "aed35d75-442a-5745-81f9-db85fcbf8be1" :question/math-academy-id "q-72840" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{4}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{4})=1.318\\,116…≈1.318\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1.318$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=1.318.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=1.318.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.318.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/8797bd2b1558e094b72d891fd344b356e5d579e4549b788bdd0507de23f171c7.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -1.318 \\\\  & =4.965\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}≈1.318\\cdot 4.965≈7\n$$\n\nto the nearest integer." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-72840/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f607558-5a7f-52df-b344-57f2f84791e9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-72840"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-121001/selection/c0ea9b23afc60e81ab10520258338c8581011d042482e538e6c06089446f4775" :answer/id #uuid "6effe6d6-ab8a-52de-b425-4f09e0201e1f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-14\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121001/selection/f61b1589742c6cbb331792dc3fb72f918885a3f935005a6ee2b4fcb0f86b9c41" :answer/id #uuid "0ed46ffa-d115-598a-9f6c-a726a7bcbeb1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-14\\,400" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121001/selection/a1af39a9db471ef15572cbb183aaf7d3b31a958483277541b89c812886c40e8c" :answer/id #uuid "c3c4fe88-429a-5f6f-a66e-38eee7fb13c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-16\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121001/selection/b16b0bc176e36e45dd90709edcd6db89ccb834489cc28f8a623a7f48fb17275b" :answer/id #uuid "3acba09f-4895-5fb2-aca8-c2caa913c81d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-10\\,400" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121001/selection/11ac5eed1544935dd7fa32bcdc19f581f91d2e2d68c2da52205aa58c1788f855" :answer/id #uuid "d5edc613-dbdb-574b-9113-c1ed74ef05a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-12\\,400" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121001/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "14997c84-1ae3-554b-8b46-bf2170865922" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-121001/selection/f61b1589742c6cbb331792dc3fb72f918885a3f935005a6ee2b4fcb0f86b9c41" :answer-field/choices ["q-121001/selection/c0ea9b23afc60e81ab10520258338c8581011d042482e538e6c06089446f4775" "q-121001/selection/f61b1589742c6cbb331792dc3fb72f918885a3f935005a6ee2b4fcb0f86b9c41" "q-121001/selection/a1af39a9db471ef15572cbb183aaf7d3b31a958483277541b89c812886c40e8c" "q-121001/selection/b16b0bc176e36e45dd90709edcd6db89ccb834489cc28f8a623a7f48fb17275b" "q-121001/selection/11ac5eed1544935dd7fa32bcdc19f581f91d2e2d68c2da52205aa58c1788f855"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-121001" :question/id #uuid "f1b641d9-36cf-5d43-ae0b-f1ea1e2a31f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-121001" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1=\\frac{3}{4}$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1 & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})={120}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${120}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={120}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/ec1135cb0a4d35130a1c11baf9f421a3cdb4bc22cc1b436686dce722fa3c812e.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}$ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{120}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\pm {120}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=120\\cdot (-120)=-14\\,400.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-121001/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26666440-d376-5cb6-b922-9850e5c42eb3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-121001"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-20214/selection/e5e20467f4ad53d124c2d4ce12a980864e847586bf9b48db9ef00ae35515cbaf" :answer/id #uuid "78b19398-ae88-5f50-9bb9-c6ccdcb712a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-20214/selection/eab6ab1b80ad86625c3221b4cea28fa41e4240c07d067fc5fdac7b9159d662e3" :answer/id #uuid "704e88fe-1d04-54dc-b82f-ddb3e0d931fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{25{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-20214/selection/b44290e44641e6fbfb8d9fe45eb9264aadc6d4801e73b4cc89ae8fae0834c012" :answer/id #uuid "94696305-6792-588e-947c-b3eafa081628" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-20214/selection/0bef786c82f91e7cf8c4585a8b2b4252fc10db4bed36e734091535a098004359" :answer/id #uuid "71f3aa1c-e063-5520-b9e6-591e43992127" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-20214/selection/6d0ddf4a027d33d63c47ea1e345b94bbfab0dc44fa0fe8d8d79232a294678632" :answer/id #uuid "98ff9ecf-7229-55af-8d76-47ef6d387e5d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-20214/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9f7fc347-a900-5431-abdf-f04ded18e1ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-20214/selection/eab6ab1b80ad86625c3221b4cea28fa41e4240c07d067fc5fdac7b9159d662e3" :answer-field/choices ["q-20214/selection/e5e20467f4ad53d124c2d4ce12a980864e847586bf9b48db9ef00ae35515cbaf" "q-20214/selection/eab6ab1b80ad86625c3221b4cea28fa41e4240c07d067fc5fdac7b9159d662e3" "q-20214/selection/b44290e44641e6fbfb8d9fe45eb9264aadc6d4801e73b4cc89ae8fae0834c012" "q-20214/selection/0bef786c82f91e7cf8c4585a8b2b4252fc10db4bed36e734091535a098004359" "q-20214/selection/6d0ddf4a027d33d63c47ea1e345b94bbfab0dc44fa0fe8d8d79232a294678632"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-20214" :question/id #uuid "79f1b09d-5a24-562d-94a3-62292789098e" :question/math-academy-id "q-20214" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3=0$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3 & =0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/6bc5aed39e6421b8ff2416edb75dc888c8f38ae14f99b3d6e28d8e4c9302908f.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi $ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}-2\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\pm \\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\cdot (-\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{25{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-20214/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26666440-d376-5cb6-b922-9850e5c42eb3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-20214"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-338688/field-1/e52b829daa8bd68fe53c085738a0d4fbb4032f675657f14b3e43001213161d96" :answer/id #uuid "dff3020a-4098-5421-b2bf-bf2fc390c8e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338688/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "855f1173-5dce-5b3f-a46a-5228eb8bd733" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-338688/field-1/e52b829daa8bd68fe53c085738a0d4fbb4032f675657f14b3e43001213161d96" :answer-field/choices ["q-338688/field-1/e52b829daa8bd68fe53c085738a0d4fbb4032f675657f14b3e43001213161d96"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338688/field-2/9f3fe0666fe667480d480b2f3c4f24f5e2ac276a7450efc6201b6fbe6a76b60c" :answer/id #uuid "115e897e-49a3-549b-8afc-b35062c87eee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338688/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "ee68c436-f0cf-54a6-9342-7644485d45dd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-338688/field-2/9f3fe0666fe667480d480b2f3c4f24f5e2ac276a7450efc6201b6fbe6a76b60c" :answer-field/choices ["q-338688/field-2/9f3fe0666fe667480d480b2f3c4f24f5e2ac276a7450efc6201b6fbe6a76b60c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338688" :question/id #uuid "22b48a48-cf0d-5b45-afdb-643b245ba807" :question/math-academy-id "q-338688" :question/problem "Arranged in **ascending order**, the solutions of the equation $\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1=0$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $ are $x=${{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/8ea1a5ca6b0dc0bd7867ab841c629f9966f2b97de4585b9c595a33af206c4a7f.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi $ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}-2\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-338688/field-1" "q-338688/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26666440-d376-5cb6-b922-9850e5c42eb3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-338688"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-274588/field-1/39d1580af5b53b3c94bed912f57b0620863ebd9f9d121bf0912afb10bf5762fe" :answer/id #uuid "b1203d39-3084-5f7f-95b7-75d57f5a894e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-112" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-274588/field-1/929d54af1ce3c37c5e2af424428326ec37432c9399788240c1ce5cf5f7c4add1" :answer/id #uuid "2cc49d79-847d-5d37-9051-c6978fa32004" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-68" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-274588/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "0aaf7035-46e4-5145-b79c-926c16bff8f0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-274588/field-1/39d1580af5b53b3c94bed912f57b0620863ebd9f9d121bf0912afb10bf5762fe" :answer-field/choices ["q-274588/field-1/39d1580af5b53b3c94bed912f57b0620863ebd9f9d121bf0912afb10bf5762fe" "q-274588/field-1/929d54af1ce3c37c5e2af424428326ec37432c9399788240c1ce5cf5f7c4add1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-274588/field-2/ad7a3824e2b90d316aedd9968e2fdff13c67ee5110546b7a43eeb384aa267fd0" :answer/id #uuid "28d228bc-6220-557f-b5e7-96edb3fbf836" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "112" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-274588/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "02cce5ad-08d7-5419-80e8-a8a4985f8621" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-274588/field-2/ad7a3824e2b90d316aedd9968e2fdff13c67ee5110546b7a43eeb384aa267fd0" :answer-field/choices ["q-274588/field-2/ad7a3824e2b90d316aedd9968e2fdff13c67ee5110546b7a43eeb384aa267fd0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-274588" :question/id #uuid "efec1dda-577f-5294-a47d-8a3cd24247de" :question/math-academy-id "q-274588" :question/problem "Arranged in **ascending order** and rounded to **the nearest integer**, the solutions of the equation $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1=\\frac{1}{4}$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}$ are $x=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1 & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{3}{8})=(112.024\\,3…)^{∘}≈{112.024}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${112.024}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution, rounded to the nearest integer, is $x={112}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={67.976}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={67.976}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/18b8161029f2013671daadc367e54e4f02d1d7fac2134ea4953a725236cbddac.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{67.976}^{∘} \\\\  & ={247.976}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}$ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =X-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={247.976}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{112.024}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈-{112}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x={-112}^{∘}$ and $x={112}^{∘}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-274588/field-1" "q-274588/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26666440-d376-5cb6-b922-9850e5c42eb3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-274588"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120997/selection/04cdb3bacf67b0f2b5eae1203bba02446d9a81e13eee8b0b94da196dd5389b3e" :answer/id #uuid "96f6d6e3-0bd3-5882-a560-864cf3b9d86e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120997/selection/a1e6b6a5d9037ab5b5300c19c375d8395cb38a4c99d0c9967d77d351c49c78f8" :answer/id #uuid "02b77fdb-e76e-5852-8333-e57cdd897182" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1\\,050" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120997/selection/13beeef43f0f1d83d4c9527a4eacea9652a753ec10f3c23183a6971ffb44ebe2" :answer/id #uuid "da7cb799-d005-5b30-8db5-96a733a4eb9e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4\\,050" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120997/selection/493e3cd55dec2569124a68a92bcd04f77f0691927ce0ce8355ec34a4fb12ef0c" :answer/id #uuid "79be831e-5882-5d90-a89e-9c1c35d3dfce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\,050" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120997/selection/b9fc17e6a09e576eaad401b7ccb1d01fa4f122f876f47fb90de40bf5ca3fb55d" :answer/id #uuid "8db63621-d061-5a45-bad1-c640de376009" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\,025" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120997/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e657490f-35bc-5404-8f3a-2bde413c1805" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120997/selection/b9fc17e6a09e576eaad401b7ccb1d01fa4f122f876f47fb90de40bf5ca3fb55d" :answer-field/choices ["q-120997/selection/04cdb3bacf67b0f2b5eae1203bba02446d9a81e13eee8b0b94da196dd5389b3e" "q-120997/selection/a1e6b6a5d9037ab5b5300c19c375d8395cb38a4c99d0c9967d77d351c49c78f8" "q-120997/selection/13beeef43f0f1d83d4c9527a4eacea9652a753ec10f3c23183a6971ffb44ebe2" "q-120997/selection/493e3cd55dec2569124a68a92bcd04f77f0691927ce0ce8355ec34a4fb12ef0c" "q-120997/selection/b9fc17e6a09e576eaad401b7ccb1d01fa4f122f876f47fb90de40bf5ca3fb55d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120997" :question/id #uuid "7de16d9d-6113-58f0-8744-682ffcd73ecf" :question/math-academy-id "q-120997" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3\\sqrt{2}=0$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3\\sqrt{2} & =0 \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${45}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={45}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/c722d4fa2b77011e4df5b086c19bb09943446941484cfaf87559ca39a52183bc.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}$ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{45}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\pm {45}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=45\\cdot (-45)=-2\\,025.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-120997/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26666440-d376-5cb6-b922-9850e5c42eb3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120997"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-338738/field-1/0e4e79001619645600fe317a8a7dc3e7c54e40dedca3eec2190744766458f0b4" :answer/id #uuid "4923efdd-f65c-5aee-9ea6-c386d4f9856e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338738/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a8c06654-cb97-5bb4-9ae4-62881b7893e8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-338738/field-1/0e4e79001619645600fe317a8a7dc3e7c54e40dedca3eec2190744766458f0b4" :answer-field/choices ["q-338738/field-1/0e4e79001619645600fe317a8a7dc3e7c54e40dedca3eec2190744766458f0b4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338738/field-2/e7e505d325c3cadeeeaf26a0afda20aaa10bea508f9739931632d1057683cd77" :answer/id #uuid "5adb5fa4-e92b-5241-8a37-c99a6451b6fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338738/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "82d55449-66ed-5796-abc8-c8c6dcd2ba2d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-338738/field-2/e7e505d325c3cadeeeaf26a0afda20aaa10bea508f9739931632d1057683cd77" :answer-field/choices ["q-338738/field-2/e7e505d325c3cadeeeaf26a0afda20aaa10bea508f9739931632d1057683cd77"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338738" :question/id #uuid "92118efb-6092-5861-938f-35cfcc896fb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-338738" :question/problem "Arranged in **ascending order**, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $ are $x=${{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ correspond to the roots of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find all solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its roots at\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the second solution ${x}_{2}$ in the given range, we set $n=-1$ and we get\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions of the equation, arranged in ascending order, are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-338738/field-1" "q-338738/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9f482ac-9771-52a3-9517-8494c3de6379"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-338738"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-72843/selection/3e954aec7b654f9f4851cd71985b22696f91f19abb97227c921711e0fe801d28" :answer/id #uuid "d5ade304-0307-52af-890d-37adafa8a04c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x={60}^{∘},\\,{180}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72843/selection/3a42611fb0ffec076b8c9d7a306225c40830435f83a892a8e1231323e05caf6a" :answer/id #uuid "f35daf13-716b-58d4-98b9-88fca216f79d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x={90}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72843/selection/9a1e3a89b30afb62da9d9b8c991823e5db06ffe27c7d30d34f15deffb7bd1626" :answer/id #uuid "5fc4900d-2a15-5533-bc48-6fc1b9f898d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72843/selection/135ddd24e19ef3138bca0150914abaef384649661c301b69fe1758743889a3ef" :answer/id #uuid "43703d42-0970-5c9a-a5ca-d96c83bb26bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x={180}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72843/selection/acc36777bd8a937d065ee4cbf54fe05b363c5c1a2a85d2d8b8abd3b238959a71" :answer/id #uuid "98e7f342-3eca-55c4-886d-a453ee824c0a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x={270}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72843/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2b583a18-f6cb-53fa-b23a-abf13a9c85c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-72843/selection/9a1e3a89b30afb62da9d9b8c991823e5db06ffe27c7d30d34f15deffb7bd1626" :answer-field/choices ["q-72843/selection/3e954aec7b654f9f4851cd71985b22696f91f19abb97227c921711e0fe801d28" "q-72843/selection/3a42611fb0ffec076b8c9d7a306225c40830435f83a892a8e1231323e05caf6a" "q-72843/selection/9a1e3a89b30afb62da9d9b8c991823e5db06ffe27c7d30d34f15deffb7bd1626" "q-72843/selection/135ddd24e19ef3138bca0150914abaef384649661c301b69fe1758743889a3ef" "q-72843/selection/acc36777bd8a937d065ee4cbf54fe05b363c5c1a2a85d2d8b8abd3b238959a71"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-72843" :question/id #uuid "9cf73f95-3551-5a58-91c6-cbb7c7b5cef9" :question/math-academy-id "q-72843" :question/problem "Solve the equation $2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=2$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =2 \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ correspond to the roots of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${90}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its roots at\n\n$$\nx={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}\\cdot n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the other solution ${x}_{2}$ in the given range, we set $n=1,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}={270}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-72843/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9f482ac-9771-52a3-9517-8494c3de6379"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-72843"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-121088/selection/f4b43ecca675606167d6716b42d9f5f32842579cb5f8e81238f96dd069b7b382" :answer/id #uuid "0b67ab78-ce97-5c92-963a-439249374587" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x={0}^{∘},\\,{180}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121088/selection/2678de7ba47ceec30ebd0d5fd01e1c3bd7e507e0021f9d611d824d4c57be4c21" :answer/id #uuid "46683718-66a2-556a-b62b-15e6122811cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x={0}^{∘},\\,{90}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121088/selection/3daf0487e04cc4c2b369692c65693a0d8b66011a44610fd4eb04aefe8323bff4" :answer/id #uuid "784cc398-0b44-5d37-ba95-30aac8871b31" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x={0}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121088/selection/de948c06b3024da1f68bf0313916d7b3a22542384514c4684be784c1f405968a" :answer/id #uuid "b58d3212-f558-53e3-b1a2-34b714ebdabf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-{90}^{∘},\\,{90}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121088/selection/135ddd24e19ef3138bca0150914abaef384649661c301b69fe1758743889a3ef" :answer/id #uuid "75b4922f-efb3-57ca-a6cb-bd84971a6382" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x={180}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121088/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "12281a27-ce6e-5e5f-a075-9abec95e86f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-121088/selection/3daf0487e04cc4c2b369692c65693a0d8b66011a44610fd4eb04aefe8323bff4" :answer-field/choices ["q-121088/selection/f4b43ecca675606167d6716b42d9f5f32842579cb5f8e81238f96dd069b7b382" "q-121088/selection/2678de7ba47ceec30ebd0d5fd01e1c3bd7e507e0021f9d611d824d4c57be4c21" "q-121088/selection/3daf0487e04cc4c2b369692c65693a0d8b66011a44610fd4eb04aefe8323bff4" "q-121088/selection/de948c06b3024da1f68bf0313916d7b3a22542384514c4684be784c1f405968a" "q-121088/selection/135ddd24e19ef3138bca0150914abaef384649661c301b69fe1758743889a3ef"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-121088" :question/id #uuid "834a030a-e5b9-51bb-9d9c-9c0ebbe3f880" :question/math-academy-id "q-121088" :question/problem "Solve the equation $1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=2$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1$ correspond to the values of $x$ at which $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ attains its maximum value.\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1)={0}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${0}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={0}^{∘}.$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its maximum values at\n\n$$\nx={360}^{∘}\\cdot n.\n$$\n\nSince the domain $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}$ is one period, and we have already found one maximum value in this period, we conclude that $x={0}^{∘}$ is the only solution." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-121088/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9f482ac-9771-52a3-9517-8494c3de6379"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-121088"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-338744/field-1/f37b140e44c3c1469a0b674232c5aaeb4f71e6e7b8d79e0c213b062c66caacc7" :answer/id #uuid "da7731db-8d85-57ff-92cb-ab6a7ea0ffb3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338744/field-1/29ec2dfefa1bd3742bf5ba922b9c261dd5f2325bd8484e9153eb979dbd6eced6" :answer/id #uuid "c3ade93d-a2f2-5836-abfc-384acec5ff8b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338744/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "8bfbe3b8-005d-5366-ab11-e52f9f0ddf60" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-338744/field-1/f37b140e44c3c1469a0b674232c5aaeb4f71e6e7b8d79e0c213b062c66caacc7" :answer-field/choices ["q-338744/field-1/f37b140e44c3c1469a0b674232c5aaeb4f71e6e7b8d79e0c213b062c66caacc7" "q-338744/field-1/29ec2dfefa1bd3742bf5ba922b9c261dd5f2325bd8484e9153eb979dbd6eced6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338744" :question/id #uuid "67443d1b-c390-5dbe-9010-f3276669865e" :question/math-academy-id "q-338744" :question/problem "The solution to the equation $3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3=0$ for $\\pi \\le x<3\\pi $ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3 & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1$ correspond to the values of $x$ at which $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ attains its maximum value.\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0$ does not lie inside the required domain, we must find other solutions.\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its maximum values at\n\n$$\nx=2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nTo find a solution in the given range, we set $n=1,$ and we get\n\n$$\nx=2\\pi .\n$$\n\nSince the domain $\\pi \\le x<3\\pi $ is one period, and we have already found one maximum value in this period, we conclude that $x=2\\pi $ is the only solution." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-338744/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9f482ac-9771-52a3-9517-8494c3de6379"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-338744"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-121084/selection/6bac05a62229f29eba4498544257eb386a58da0ea736d3d95ca346c883c43950" :answer/id #uuid "11694741-3022-5b74-883c-be55f5c9a4d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121084/selection/2446a56c622d9853838e13c4f43741e1897d4df1c9bc4d78a8506d9bcfbdac93" :answer/id #uuid "e1d64d19-11a7-57fe-aa72-e44a8bbdbf9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=0,\\,\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121084/selection/a16ab02c17ac91ffa05f0a534c1f52fca60f1c2594f77a754cae2066c038b929" :answer/id #uuid "6dae9ee0-804a-5964-a66c-81b53b665115" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121084/selection/4474a39579e8640865737ab970e0c30d067a600377b903df4b98790832a3d86e" :answer/id #uuid "e7076f62-2b3c-5fd5-875d-2ca2b064c12e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121084/selection/c143ca64ec013e5fe4018485f8c456b26425ea6baf2691e509470a46cd90ac4d" :answer/id #uuid "4f560dfe-f352-5811-a23e-22b9d94a26b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121084/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b62952e8-cf53-52cf-afce-e56797d09219" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-121084/selection/6bac05a62229f29eba4498544257eb386a58da0ea736d3d95ca346c883c43950" :answer-field/choices ["q-121084/selection/6bac05a62229f29eba4498544257eb386a58da0ea736d3d95ca346c883c43950" "q-121084/selection/2446a56c622d9853838e13c4f43741e1897d4df1c9bc4d78a8506d9bcfbdac93" "q-121084/selection/a16ab02c17ac91ffa05f0a534c1f52fca60f1c2594f77a754cae2066c038b929" "q-121084/selection/4474a39579e8640865737ab970e0c30d067a600377b903df4b98790832a3d86e" "q-121084/selection/c143ca64ec013e5fe4018485f8c456b26425ea6baf2691e509470a46cd90ac4d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-121084" :question/id #uuid "6f2a7b85-3ffa-58fb-9d53-158260676a5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-121084" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4}{5}=1$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4}{5} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =5-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1$ correspond to the values of $x$ at which $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ attains its maximum value.\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.$ \n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its maximum values at\n\n$$\nx=2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSince the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is one period, and we have already found one maximum value in this period, we conclude that $x=0$ is the only solution." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-121084/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9f482ac-9771-52a3-9517-8494c3de6379"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-121084"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
